From 2104b6891ca2e8d17f0e7f6acb3e09cb3a8ff39c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Oleksii Zakharov <contact@zalexdev.com>
Date: Sat, 15 Aug 2026 21:42:56 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] um: build against bionic (Android NDK)

UML assumes glibc in more places than it looks, and an Android phone is a
host with the right kernel and the wrong libc. Building against the NDK
turns up seven independent problems, one of which explains most of the
others: USER_CFLAGS adds the kernel's include directory with -idirafter,
so a sysroot shipping its own linux/compiler_types.h shadows the kernel's.
The NDK's does -- a 265-byte uapi stub defining neither __section nor
__used. Force-include compiler_attributes.h instead, and not
compiler_types.h, which defines __private, a struct member name in
bionic's bits/pthread_types.h.

The rest: probe for -lutil, -lrt and -lpthread rather than assuming
glibc's split, because bionic folds all three into libc and naming them
fails the link outright; drop the stdio stdout/stderr references, which
pull copy relocations UML's linker script cannot align; pthread_cancel
does not exist in bionic; align .dynbss; and stub_exe= to exec the stub
from a path, since a memfd is not always available.

Two things that only show up at runtime. The linker script declared no
PHDRS, so lld gave each permission group its own PT_LOAD taking addresses
from the script, and any group starting mid-page produced two segments
sharing a page. load_elf_binary() maps ET_EXEC segments with
MAP_FIXED_NOREPLACE, so the second mmap of that page returns -EEXIST, and
because this happens past the point of no return the kernel can only
SIGSEGV the process: a bare "Segmentation fault" with an unmapped PC. bfd
ld merged the groups into one RWE segment and the question never arose.
And Android tags pointers, so the stack address get_top_address() reads
carries a top byte that must be masked before it means anything.

Finally, the names the kernel defines that bionic's libc also references
are renamed on the object files at link time. In a static link the linker
is happy to satisfy libc's reference with the kernel's definition, and
sched_getaffinity showed the shape of that: bionic's start-up reached the
kernel's implementation and dereferenced a task_struct that did not exist
yet, with nothing warning, because libc only references these and there is
no duplicate-definition error to notice. objcopy --redefine-syms renames
definitions and references together; doing it with -D would also rewrite
these names where other headers paste them into identifiers.

Signed-off-by: Oleksii Zakharov <contact@zalexdev.com>
---
 arch/arm64/Makefile.um                  |  47 ++++++
 arch/arm64/um/asm/string.h              |  51 ++++++
 arch/arm64/um/bionic-syms.redef         |  42 +++++
 arch/um/Makefile                        |  10 ++
 arch/um/drivers/Kconfig                 |  21 ++-
 arch/um/include/shared/as-layout.h      |  29 ++++
 arch/um/include/shared/init.h           |  22 +++
 arch/um/include/shared/skas/mm_id.h     |  13 ++
 arch/um/include/shared/skas/skas.h      |   7 +
 arch/um/include/shared/skas/stub-data.h |  25 ++-
 arch/um/kernel/dyn.lds.S                |  10 ++
 arch/um/kernel/skas/Makefile            |   9 +-
 arch/um/kernel/skas/mmu.c               |  10 +-
 arch/um/kernel/um_arch.c                |  63 ++++++-
 arch/um/kernel/uml.lds.S                |  73 +++++++-
 arch/um/os-Linux/file.c                 |   8 +-
 arch/um/os-Linux/helper.c               |  16 ++
 arch/um/os-Linux/skas/process.c         | 148 +++++++++++++++-
 arch/um/os-Linux/start_up.c             | 216 ++++++++++++++++++++----
 arch/um/os-Linux/util.c                 |  30 +++-
 scripts/link-vmlinux.sh                 |  33 +++-
 21 files changed, 832 insertions(+), 51 deletions(-)
 create mode 100644 arch/arm64/um/bionic-syms.redef

diff --git a/arch/arm64/Makefile.um b/arch/arm64/Makefile.um
index 389ec45eb6b2e..9f128e5bd4eb0 100644
--- a/arch/arm64/Makefile.um
+++ b/arch/arm64/Makefile.um
@@ -23,3 +23,50 @@ CHECKFLAGS	+= -D__aarch64__
 # -mgeneral-regs-only would break the host-side code that legitimately does.
 
 LINK-$(CONFIG_LD_SCRIPT_DYN_RPATH) += -Wl,-rpath,/lib
+
+# Building against bionic (the Android NDK) collides with the kernel's own
+# definitions of a set of standard-library names. glibc does not show this:
+# its libc.a is finely split, so the objects carrying memcpy, snprintf and the
+# rest are simply never pulled in once the kernel's definitions satisfy every
+# reference. bionic's libc.a is coarser -- the objects that *are* needed also
+# define these -- and the static link then fails with twenty duplicate symbols.
+#
+# Resolving it with --allow-multiple-definition is worse than not building:
+# link order puts the kernel first, so libc's internals end up calling the
+# kernel's memcpy and sem_init, and the binary segfaults before main().
+#
+# The string family is handled in arch/arm64/um/asm/string.h instead, by
+# declaring __HAVE_ARCH_* so lib/string.c does not compile its own -- renaming
+# those fights CONFIG_FORTIFY_SOURCE, which redefines them as macros.
+#
+# The rest are renamed, which is exactly what UML already does for
+# errno, sigprocmask, mktime and strrchr in arch/um/Makefile. These defines
+# reach kernel objects only, never USER_OBJS, so host-side code and libc keep
+# the real names. Compiler-emitted implicit calls (struct copies lowering to
+# memcpy) also keep the real name and get libc's implementation, which is
+# correct and usually faster than the kernel's generic C.
+#
+# The list is not guesswork and not the duplicate-symbol errors either. Those
+# only catch names both sides *define*; the dangerous case is a name libc
+# merely *references* while the kernel defines it, because then there is no
+# error at all -- the reference simply binds to the kernel's function. That is
+# what happened with sched_getaffinity: bionic's startup called it, got the
+# kernel's, and dereferenced a task_struct that does not exist yet. The symptom
+# was a SIGSEGV writing to 0x80 with no output.
+#
+# So the list is the intersection of "undefined in bionic's libc.a" with
+# "defined in vmlinux.a", computed with llvm-nm:
+#
+#   llvm-nm --undefined-only libc.a         | sort -u > A
+#   llvm-nm --defined-only --extern-only vmlinux.a | sort -u > B
+#   comm -12 A B
+#
+# Detected from the compiler rather than configured, because the whole
+# difference is which libc the target triple selects.
+UM_BIONIC := $(shell echo | $(CC) $(CLANG_FLAGS) -dM -E - 2>/dev/null | grep -c __ANDROID__)
+
+ifeq ($(UM_BIONIC),1)
+# Renamed on the object files at link time, not here: see
+# arch/arm64/um/bionic-syms.redef for the list and for why -D is the wrong tool.
+export UM_REDEF_SYMS := $(srctree)/arch/arm64/um/bionic-syms.redef
+endif
diff --git a/arch/arm64/um/asm/string.h b/arch/arm64/um/asm/string.h
index ff82f2c825acb..437043633611a 100644
--- a/arch/arm64/um/asm/string.h
+++ b/arch/arm64/um/asm/string.h
@@ -15,4 +15,55 @@
  * the libc that the UML binary is linked against.
  */
 
+
+/*
+ * Against bionic, let libc supply these.
+ *
+ * The comment above explains why no __HAVE_ARCH_* is declared for a glibc
+ * build: arch/arm64/lib is not compiled for ARCH=um, so claiming the arch has
+ * its own would leave the symbols undefined. Against bionic the situation is
+ * the opposite -- libc.a certainly has them, and its objects get pulled in
+ * regardless because bionic's libc is coarser-grained than glibc's, so the
+ * kernel's copies in lib/string.c collide with libc's at static link time.
+ *
+ * Declaring them here stops lib/string.c compiling its own, which removes the
+ * collision at the source rather than renaming one side of it. Renaming is the
+ * wrong tool for this family in particular: CONFIG_FORTIFY_SOURCE redefines
+ * these names as macros, so a -Dstrchr=kernel_strchr fights the fortify
+ * wrappers and produces "call to undeclared library function 'strchr'".
+ */
+#ifdef __ANDROID__
+#define __HAVE_ARCH_MEMCPY
+#define __HAVE_ARCH_MEMSET
+#define __HAVE_ARCH_MEMMOVE
+#define __HAVE_ARCH_MEMCMP
+#define __HAVE_ARCH_MEMCHR
+#define __HAVE_ARCH_STRCMP
+#define __HAVE_ARCH_STRCPY
+#define __HAVE_ARCH_STRCHR
+#define __HAVE_ARCH_STRCHRNUL
+#define __HAVE_ARCH_STRLEN
+#define __HAVE_ARCH_STRNCMP
+#define __HAVE_ARCH_STRNLEN
+
+/*
+ * __HAVE_ARCH_* tells <linux/string.h> that the architecture declares these,
+ * so it stops declaring them itself. Here "the architecture" is libc, so
+ * declare them with the signatures libc uses and let the link resolve them
+ * there.
+ */
+void *memcpy(void *, const void *, __kernel_size_t);
+void *memset(void *, int, __kernel_size_t);
+void *memmove(void *, const void *, __kernel_size_t);
+int memcmp(const void *, const void *, __kernel_size_t);
+void *memchr(const void *, int, __kernel_size_t);
+int strcmp(const char *, const char *);
+char *strcpy(char *, const char *);
+char *strchr(const char *, int);
+char *strchrnul(const char *, int);
+__kernel_size_t strlen(const char *);
+int strncmp(const char *, const char *, __kernel_size_t);
+__kernel_size_t strnlen(const char *, __kernel_size_t);
+#endif
+
 #endif /* __UM_ARM64_STRING_H */
diff --git a/arch/arm64/um/bionic-syms.redef b/arch/arm64/um/bionic-syms.redef
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000000..446e8e37fa8f6
--- /dev/null
+++ b/arch/arm64/um/bionic-syms.redef
@@ -0,0 +1,42 @@
+# Kernel symbols renamed when linking against bionic.
+#
+# These are names the kernel defines and bionic's libc also references. In a
+# static link the linker is happy to satisfy libc's reference with the kernel's
+# definition, and the result is libc calling into the kernel: sched_getaffinity
+# was the one that showed the shape of the problem, with bionic's start-up
+# reaching the kernel's implementation and dereferencing a task_struct that does
+# not exist yet. Nothing warns -- libc only references these, so there is no
+# duplicate-definition error to notice.
+#
+# Renaming happens on the object files rather than with -D. The preprocessor
+# does not know the difference between a function name and a token being pasted
+# into an identifier: -Dsnprintf=kernel_snprintf also rewrites the bare
+# "snprintf" inside __BPF_FUNC_MAPPER, so kernel/bpf/disasm.c ends up asking for
+# BPF_FUNC_kernel_snprintf and CONFIG_BPF_SYSCALL will not build. objcopy
+# renames definitions and references together and cannot make that mistake.
+#
+# Derived, not guessed:
+#   llvm-nm --undefined-only libc.a                > A
+#   llvm-nm --defined-only --extern-only vmlinux.a > B
+#   comm -12 A B
+snprintf kernel_snprintf
+vsnprintf kernel_vsnprintf
+sprintf kernel_sprintf
+vsprintf kernel_vsprintf
+sscanf kernel_sscanf
+vsscanf kernel_vsscanf
+stpcpy kernel_stpcpy
+getrusage kernel_getrusage
+sem_init kernel_sem_init
+timer_delete kernel_timer_delete
+sched_setaffinity kernel_sched_setaffinity
+sched_getaffinity kernel_sched_getaffinity
+sched_setscheduler kernel_sched_setscheduler
+abort kernel_abort
+strcasecmp kernel_strcasecmp
+strncasecmp kernel_strncasecmp
+strcspn kernel_strcspn
+strlcat kernel_strlcat
+strpbrk kernel_strpbrk
+strsep kernel_strsep
+strstr kernel_strstr
diff --git a/arch/um/Makefile b/arch/um/Makefile
index f505cd3d3086e..c95b4a8384686 100644
--- a/arch/um/Makefile
+++ b/arch/um/Makefile
@@ -70,6 +70,15 @@ KBUILD_AFLAGS += $(ARCH_INCLUDE)
 # are the same, and i386 rides on the -m32 in KBUILD_CFLAGS. An arm64 subarch has
 # no -m flag to ride on, so the triple has to be propagated explicitly.
 # CLANG_FLAGS is empty under gcc, so this is a no-op there.
+# The kernel's own compiler attributes are force-included rather than left to
+# -idirafter below. That flag places $(srctree)/include *after* the system
+# include directories, so <linux/compiler_types.h> resolves to the host
+# sysroot's copy whenever one exists. Debian's does not, so the kernel's is
+# found and everything works; the Android NDK's sysroot ships a 265-byte uapi
+# stub of that name, which then wins and defines none of __section, __used,
+# __aligned, noinline or the sparse annotations. The failures land far from the
+# cause -- "expected parameter declarator" on every __init in UML -- so force
+# the right definitions in instead of discovering them one macro at a time.
 USER_CFLAGS = $(patsubst $(KERNEL_DEFINES),,$(patsubst -I%,,$(KBUILD_CFLAGS))) \
 		$(CLANG_FLAGS) \
 		$(ARCH_INCLUDE) $(MODE_INCLUDE) $(filter -I%,$(CFLAGS)) \
@@ -77,6 +86,7 @@ USER_CFLAGS = $(patsubst $(KERNEL_DEFINES),,$(patsubst -I%,,$(KBUILD_CFLAGS))) \
 		-idirafter $(objtree)/include -D__KERNEL__ -D__UM_HOST__ \
 		-include $(srctree)/include/linux/compiler-version.h \
 		-include $(srctree)/include/linux/kconfig.h \
+		-include $(srctree)/include/linux/compiler_attributes.h \
 		-idirafter $(ARCH_DIR)/include/generated
 
 #This will adjust *FLAGS accordingly to the platform.
diff --git a/arch/um/drivers/Kconfig b/arch/um/drivers/Kconfig
index 6a0354ca032fb..79a6dfd43b510 100644
--- a/arch/um/drivers/Kconfig
+++ b/arch/um/drivers/Kconfig
@@ -126,7 +126,26 @@ menu "UML Network Devices"
 
 config UML_NET_VECTOR
 	bool "Vector I/O high performance network devices"
-	select MAY_HAVE_RUNTIME_DEPS
+	#
+	# Deliberately not "select MAY_HAVE_RUNTIME_DEPS".
+	#
+	# That select existed because two of this driver's transports resolve
+	# hostnames with getaddrinfo(), which in a statically linked glibc binary
+	# may need NSS modules at run time. But it applied to the whole driver,
+	# and so made CONFIG_STATIC_LINK unavailable to anyone enabling vector
+	# networking at all -- including the transports that resolve nothing.
+	#
+	# getaddrinfo() appears exactly twice, both in user_init_socket_fds(),
+	# reached only by the gre and l2tpv3 transports. The tap, raw, bess, vde
+	# and fd transports never call it. And where it is called, a static
+	# binary degrades rather than breaks: numeric addresses resolve without
+	# NSS, and a name that needs it fails with an EAI_ error like any other
+	# lookup failure.
+	#
+	# The combination this was blocking is the only way to have networking at
+	# all on a host with no glibc, which is to say on Android: the binary has
+	# to be static to run there, and the fd transport plus an unprivileged
+	# userspace network stack is what gives an unrooted guest a network.
 	help
 	  This User-Mode Linux network driver uses multi-message send
 	  and receive functions. The host running the UML guest must have
diff --git a/arch/um/include/shared/as-layout.h b/arch/um/include/shared/as-layout.h
index 02ef258e3395e..fcc3c339160c0 100644
--- a/arch/um/include/shared/as-layout.h
+++ b/arch/um/include/shared/as-layout.h
@@ -23,7 +23,36 @@
 #define STUB_START stub_start
 #define STUB_CODE STUB_START
 #define STUB_DATA (STUB_CODE + UM_KERN_PAGE_SIZE)
+/*
+ * One page of bookkeeping plus a signal stack. In SECCOMP mode the host kernel
+ * writes a complete signal frame onto that stack when it delivers SIGSYS to the
+ * stub, so it has to be big enough for the largest frame this host can produce.
+ *
+ * On x86 one page is comfortable. On arm64 it is not: sizeof(mcontext_t) alone
+ * is 4384 bytes, because the architecture reserves __reserved[4096] for the
+ * FPSIMD/SVE/SME records, and a measured frame is 4576 bytes -- larger than a
+ * 4 KiB page before siginfo is even counted. arm64 publishes the real
+ * requirement in AT_MINSIGSTKSZ, which is 4720 on a plain Cortex-A76 and 9984
+ * on a host with 512-bit SVE, so the size is a property of the host rather than
+ * a constant that can be measured once.
+ *
+ * Four pages therefore, giving a 12 KiB stack at 4 KiB pages, which covers
+ * every AT_MINSIGSTKSZ seen so far with room to spare. At 16 KiB pages a single
+ * page is already larger than that, so the layout is unchanged there.
+ *
+ * A power of two is required, not merely tidy: init_new_context() allocates
+ * this with __get_free_pages(..., ilog2(STUB_DATA_PAGES)), so a non-power-of-two
+ * would silently allocate fewer pages than the structure occupies.
+ *
+ * The remaining risk -- a host whose AT_MINSIGSTKSZ exceeds even this -- is not
+ * papered over: check_stub_sigstack() compares the two at boot and refuses
+ * SECCOMP mode with a message naming both numbers.
+ */
+#if defined(__aarch64__) && UM_KERN_PAGE_SIZE < 16384
+#define STUB_DATA_PAGES 4
+#else
 #define STUB_DATA_PAGES 2
+#endif
 #define STUB_SIZE ((1 + STUB_DATA_PAGES) * UM_KERN_PAGE_SIZE)
 #define STUB_END (STUB_START + STUB_SIZE)
 
diff --git a/arch/um/include/shared/init.h b/arch/um/include/shared/init.h
index 1a659e2e8cc38..16d14b45fa8d9 100644
--- a/arch/um/include/shared/init.h
+++ b/arch/um/include/shared/init.h
@@ -43,6 +43,28 @@ typedef void (*exitcall_t)(void);
 
 #include <linux/compiler_types.h>
 
+/*
+ * All this header needs from the kernel's compiler attributes is __section and
+ * __used, and reaching them through <linux/compiler_types.h> is not reliable
+ * here. This header is also used by the USER_CFLAGS objects, which compile
+ * against the host's sysroot rather than with -nostdinc, so a sysroot that
+ * ships its own linux/compiler_types.h wins the include and the kernel's copy
+ * is never seen.
+ *
+ * The Android NDK does exactly that: its sysroot carries a 265-byte uapi stub
+ * of that name which defines neither macro. The include then succeeds, __init
+ * expands to a bare __section(...), and every __init in UML becomes "expected
+ * parameter declarator" -- an error that says nothing about the real cause.
+ * The host glibc sysroot has no such file, which is why this only appears when
+ * building against bionic.
+ */
+#ifndef __section
+#define __section(s)	__attribute__((__section__(s)))
+#endif
+#ifndef __used
+#define __used		__attribute__((__used__))
+#endif
+
 /* These are for everybody (although not all archs will actually
    discard it in modules) */
 #define __init		__section(".init.text")
diff --git a/arch/um/include/shared/skas/mm_id.h b/arch/um/include/shared/skas/mm_id.h
index 18c0621430d2c..49d3b8b54ab0f 100644
--- a/arch/um/include/shared/skas/mm_id.h
+++ b/arch/um/include/shared/skas/mm_id.h
@@ -21,6 +21,19 @@ struct mm_id {
 	int syscall_fd_map[STUB_MAX_FDS];
 };
 
+/*
+ * Sparse's lock annotations, normally from <linux/compiler_types.h>. This
+ * header is reached by USER_CFLAGS objects too, and a sysroot that ships its
+ * own linux/compiler_types.h shadows the kernel's -- the Android NDK does.
+ * Without these the declarations below parse as function definitions.
+ */
+#ifndef __acquires
+#define __acquires(x)
+#endif
+#ifndef __releases
+#define __releases(x)
+#endif
+
 struct mutex *__get_turnstile(struct mm_id *mm_id);
 void enter_turnstile(struct mm_id *mm_id) __acquires(__get_turnstile(mm_id));
 void exit_turnstile(struct mm_id *mm_id) __releases(__get_turnstile(mm_id));
diff --git a/arch/um/include/shared/skas/skas.h b/arch/um/include/shared/skas/skas.h
index 2237ffedec75b..b2374c7938a70 100644
--- a/arch/um/include/shared/skas/skas.h
+++ b/arch/um/include/shared/skas/skas.h
@@ -10,6 +10,13 @@
 
 extern int using_seccomp;
 
+/*
+ * Whether the host implements PTRACE_SYSEMU. Where it does not, UML builds the
+ * same semantics out of PTRACE_SYSCALL and a syscall cancellation; see
+ * check_sysemu(). Set once at boot, before any stub exists.
+ */
+extern int have_ptrace_sysemu;
+
 extern void new_thread_handler(void);
 extern void handle_syscall(struct uml_pt_regs *regs);
 extern unsigned long current_stub_stack(void);
diff --git a/arch/um/include/shared/skas/stub-data.h b/arch/um/include/shared/skas/stub-data.h
index 27db38e95df91..d9bca64c3e93c 100644
--- a/arch/um/include/shared/skas/stub-data.h
+++ b/arch/um/include/shared/skas/stub-data.h
@@ -14,6 +14,19 @@
 #include <sysdep/stub-data.h>
 #include <mm_id.h>
 
+/* See the note in skas/mm_id.h: a sysroot's linux/compiler_types.h can shadow
+ * the kernel's, leaving the attribute macros undefined for USER_CFLAGS objects.
+ */
+#ifndef __aligned
+#define __aligned(x)	__attribute__((__aligned__(x)))
+#endif
+#ifndef __section
+#define __section(x)	__attribute__((__section__(x)))
+#endif
+#ifndef __used
+#define __used		__attribute__((__used__))
+#endif
+
 #define FUTEX_IN_CHILD 0
 #define FUTEX_IN_KERN 1
 
@@ -69,8 +82,16 @@ struct stub_data {
 	/* seccomp architecture specific state restore */
 	struct stub_data_arch arch_data;
 
-	/* Stack for our signal handlers and for calling into . */
-	unsigned char sigstack[UM_KERN_PAGE_SIZE] __aligned(UM_KERN_PAGE_SIZE);
+	/*
+	 * Stack for our signal handlers and for calling into .
+	 *
+	 * Sized from STUB_DATA_PAGES rather than fixed at one page: in SECCOMP
+	 * mode the host writes a whole signal frame here, and on arm64 that
+	 * frame is larger than a 4 KiB page. See the comment on STUB_DATA_PAGES
+	 * in as-layout.h.
+	 */
+	unsigned char sigstack[(STUB_DATA_PAGES - 1) * UM_KERN_PAGE_SIZE]
+		__aligned(UM_KERN_PAGE_SIZE);
 };
 
 #endif
diff --git a/arch/um/kernel/dyn.lds.S b/arch/um/kernel/dyn.lds.S
index b88bd144d5ac0..cefd7e6f723b6 100644
--- a/arch/um/kernel/dyn.lds.S
+++ b/arch/um/kernel/dyn.lds.S
@@ -157,6 +157,16 @@ SECTIONS
   PROVIDE (edata = .);
   .bss            : {
    __bss_start = .;
+   /*
+    * Copy relocations land in .dynbss, and with an explicit linker script the
+    * linker places them at the current location rather than re-aligning for
+    * them. A libc whose exported data needs 8-byte alignment then fails to
+    * link: bionic's stderr is a pointer, and lld rejects the reference with
+    * "improper alignment for relocation R_AARCH64_LDST64_ABS_LO12_NC".
+    * glibc does not show this because UML has historically been linked
+    * statically against it here, and a static link has no copy relocations.
+    */
+   . = ALIGN(16);
    *(.dynbss)
    *(.bss .bss.* .gnu.linkonce.b.*)
    *(COMMON)
diff --git a/arch/um/kernel/skas/Makefile b/arch/um/kernel/skas/Makefile
index 1de51b3905187..df602bfeeb64e 100644
--- a/arch/um/kernel/skas/Makefile
+++ b/arch/um/kernel/skas/Makefile
@@ -32,7 +32,14 @@ quiet_cmd_stub_exe = STUB_EXE $@
 			   $(filter-out $(UM_GPROF_OPT) $(UM_GCOV_OPT),$(KBUILD_CFLAGS)) $(STUB_EXE_LDFLAGS) \
 			   $(filter %.o,$^)
 
-STUB_EXE_LDFLAGS = -Wl,-n -static
+# --no-rosegment: lld gives read-only data its own PT_LOAD, and with -n
+# (nmagic, no page alignment) the two segments end up sharing a page. The
+# kernel maps ET_EXEC segments with MAP_FIXED_NOREPLACE, so the second mmap of
+# that page returns -EEXIST and the exec fails after the point of no return --
+# which surfaces as the *guest* failing to start with -ENOMEM, because
+# start_userspace() cannot exec the stub. Merging rodata into the text segment
+# leaves one loadable segment, which is what -n was written for.
+STUB_EXE_LDFLAGS = -Wl,-n -Wl,--no-rosegment -static
 
 targets += stub_exe.dbg stub_exe $(stub_exe_objs-y)
 
diff --git a/arch/um/kernel/skas/mmu.c b/arch/um/kernel/skas/mmu.c
index b5017096028ba..857a9bb7e648a 100644
--- a/arch/um/kernel/skas/mmu.c
+++ b/arch/um/kernel/skas/mmu.c
@@ -50,8 +50,11 @@ int init_new_context(struct task_struct *task, struct mm_struct *mm)
 	spin_lock_init(&mm->context.sync_tlb_lock);
 
 	stack = __get_free_pages(GFP_KERNEL | __GFP_ZERO, ilog2(STUB_DATA_PAGES));
